so i just moved in so i'm still sort of  figuring out my morning routine but it  always starts with a cup of tea  my name is eliana pipes and i'm from los  angeles california  i loved growing up in la and really  growing up in la is responsible for why  i came into the arts  i was an actor and now i'm sort of  launching into a professional  screenwriting career  really writing came out of frustration  with the roles that were available to me  as an actor and so from the beginning it  was really an effort to create space for  different voices  and that was part of planting the seed  of oh i think i could do that  hi everybody oh my gosh it's so  wonderful to see you  i've been keeping a journal since i was  13 and my journal is typed  journaling is so important to me and  creative self-expression is so important  to me i think  it empowers people who aren't given  space in the world to create that space  for themselves  it also for me has been really helpful  with getting over the sort of fear of  the blank page thing  because you kind of just start typing  and then eventually i realized that  really i've started writing my project  and i just click over and there i am and  it really works  and then honestly the other big moment  that brought me into the arts was doing  academy gold  the academy gold fellowship for women  along with the wave grant was really  life-changing  and the mentor that i got on kurus has  been such a gift and she actually  suggested  pivoting the project to animation  i have never worked on an animated  project before so the prospect of  learning a new medium was so daunting  and you know for all of the incredible  difficulties that came with  this past year and with kovid and with  pivoting this production  it's also really been a gift and it  forced me to push  the storytelling forward and to push  myself forward as an artist  thank you so much i went bright red  today to match the automatic ninja how  to be done  so nails is an animated short film that  follows a young woman who develops an  unexpected crush on another girl and  when she does her fingernails start  growing at this  incredible pace which is half a blessing  because long nails are such an integral  part of black and latino beauty culture  and half a curse because short nails are  a huge part of queer culture for women  i'm black white and puerto rican and so  long nails sort of always part of my  life  it's always something that i sort of  like was proud of i'm a nail person and  then as i grew up  and discovered my queerness i also  discovered this whole new set of  signifiers  around short nails in the queer  community there's a moment in the short  where our protagonist catalina is  on her phone searching for her identity  and looking for answers about where she  belongs and who she can be  my hope for this short is that if some  real-life catalina was on her phone  looking for answers about where she  belonged  that this short would come up in the  search results and that she could watch  it and  feel seen and like she belonged  i think one of the most valuable things  that creatives can do is to  find your people then those can be the  people that you come up with  rebel rosa the storyboard artist and  character designer that i'm working with  is a friend i made in high school  we had those sort of similar challenges  that we were facing and similar  things we wanted to see that we weren't  seeing elsewhere he made every  storyboard frame  and drew over my melted goblin  storyboards to  make the beautiful pieces that ended up  in the animatic  one of the things that really draws me  to directing is the way that a director  is a leader  on set and i think i'm really interested  in arts leadership and arts advocacy and  being a leader in the culture  if we move forward with any of the  promotional things we've been thinking  of moving forward with we're going to  need an updated poster  for sure i really love exploration and  trying new things and challenging myself  it's part of  what makes art so exciting to me is is  taking on something i never thought i'd  [Music]  do  you
 

